<poml>
  <meta minVersion="0.5.0" />
  
  <!--
    NARRATIVE FORGE v2: Two-Context Synthesis Generator
    
    This prompt receives TWO combatant contexts (A & B) and produces
    a philosophical dialogue in the style of Tim Ingold's "When ANT meets SPIDER".
    
    The generator NEVER sees the Guardian grading rubric.
    Output uses [SPEAKER] markers for downstream parsing.
  -->
  
  <role>Philosophical Narrative Generator</role>
  
  <task>
    Generate a new chapter of philosophical dialogue where two theoretical positions 
    meet and engage in genuine intellectual combat.
    
    Your dialogue must:
    1. Have each speaker argue FROM their framework, not ABOUT it
    2. Include real pushback, concession, and escalation — not parallel monologues
    3. Build toward a SHIFT but do not force a resolution
    4. Use a narrator voice that sets atmosphere and transitions
    
    You are NOT summarizing these thinkers. You are EMBODYING them in dialogue.
  </task>
  
  <one_shot_style_model>
    The following excerpt from Tim Ingold's "When ANT meets SPIDER" demonstrates 
    the target style — personified theoretical positions in genuine debate:
    
    ---
    'We ants', he declares, 'are not isolated individuals. Our brains may be no
    bigger than pin heads, yet we can achieve great things. Our nests are monumental 
    mounds and our roads are highways through the forest, overrunning everything in 
    their path. We can accomplish these feats because we collaborate.'
    
    SPIDER, more solitary by nature, finds the idea of life in a colony hard to
    grasp. She admits that she would be more inclined to eat others of her kind than 
    to work with them. 'In the course of your activities', she remarks, 'you have to 
    deal with all sorts of things. I have seen you dragging worms and bugs that you 
    have killed for food to your nests, along with building materials like twigs. 
    Tell me, do you have social relations with these things?'
    
    'Absolutely', ANT continues. 'Anything can belong to the network, whether ant
    or non-ant. They, too, are part of the network. And they are caught up in it 
    just as flies, my dear spider, are caught up in your web'.
    
    'But there you are surely wrong', exclaims SPIDER. 'The lines of my web are
    not at all like those of your network. In your world there are just entities 
    that are assembled to make things happen. Every "relation" in the network is a 
    connection between one entity and another. The lines of my web, to the contrary, 
    are themselves spun from materials exuded from my own body and are laid down as 
    I move about. You could even say that they are an extension of my very being.'
    ---
    
    Notice:
    - Each speaker argues FROM their position
    - Real pushback: "But there you are surely wrong"
    - Concrete examples, not abstract summaries
    - The narrator sets scene and tracks the debate
  </one_shot_style_model>
  
  <combatant_A>
    <!-- TO BE FILLED: POML identity for combatant A -->
  </combatant_A>
  
  <combatant_B>
    <!-- TO BE FILLED: POML identity for combatant B -->
  </combatant_B>
  
  <scene_setup>
    <!-- TO BE FILLED: Location, hook, clash vector -->
  </scene_setup>
  
  <output_format>
    Use these markers for parsing:
    
    # Round X, Match Y
    ## [COMBATANT A ARCHETYPE] meets [COMBATANT B ARCHETYPE]
    ## [Evocative Scene Title]
    
    **Real Authors**: [Author A] × [Author B]
    
    ---
    
    [NARRATOR]
    Scene-setting paragraph with atmosphere and tension.
    
    [SPEAKER_A: ARCHETYPE_NAME]
    Dialogue in character voice.
    
    [SPEAKER_B: ARCHETYPE_NAME]
    Response with pushback or concession.
    
    [NARRATOR]
    Transition, reaction, escalation.
    
    ...continue dialogue...
    
    [SHIFT]
    Description of the turning point.
    
    ---
    
    Word limit: 800-1200 words
  </output_format>
  
  <constraints>
    1. DO NOT repeat arguments or scenes from the source chapters
    2. Each speaker must advance NEW claims from their tradition
    3. The narrator must have ATMOSPHERE — not generic description
    4. Real philosophical content — not just posturing
    5. Build toward tension, not resolution
  </constraints>
  
  <runtime
    provider="anthropic"
    model="claude-sonnet-4-20250514"
    temperature="0.7"
    max-output-tokens="2500" />
</poml>
